🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗗𝗡𝗦 𝗶𝘀 𝗡𝗼𝘁 𝗥𝗲𝘀𝗼𝗹𝘃𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗦𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗰𝗲 𝗡𝗮𝗺𝗲𝘀 — 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗪𝗼𝘂𝗹𝗱 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁 𝗜𝘁?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

Your application Pods are Running, but they cannot communicate with other services using Kubernetes DNS names such as "backend.default.svc.cluster.local". How would you investigate this in production?

Here’s my practical approach 👇

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟭: 𝗩𝗲𝗿𝗶𝗳𝘆 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝗗𝗡𝗦 𝗙𝗮𝗶𝗹𝘂𝗿𝗲

Run a DNS lookup from an affected Pod:

nslookup backend.default.svc.cluster.local

Check whether the issue affects one Pod, one namespace, or the entire cluster.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟮: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗖𝗼𝗿𝗲𝗗𝗡𝗦 𝗣𝗼𝗱𝘀

kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns
kubectl get svc -n kube-system

Verify that CoreDNS Pods are Running and Ready and that the "kube-dns" Service exists.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟯: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗖𝗼𝗿𝗲𝗗𝗡𝗦 𝗟𝗼𝗴𝘀

kubectl logs -n kube-system \
  -l k8s-app=kube-dns --tail=100

Look for DNS forwarding failures, upstream resolver errors, plugin issues, or repeated timeouts.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟰: 𝗩𝗲𝗿𝗶𝗳𝘆 𝗣𝗼𝗱 𝗗𝗡𝗦 𝗖𝗼𝗻𝗳𝗶𝗴𝘂𝗿𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻

kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -n <namespace> -- cat /etc/resolv.conf

Check the nameserver, search domains, and DNS options.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟱: 𝗧𝗲𝘀𝘁 𝗦𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗰𝗲 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗗𝗡𝗦 𝗥𝗲𝘀𝗼𝗹𝘂𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻

kubectl get svc backend -n default
kubectl get endpointslices -n default

Check whether the Service exists and has the expected endpoints.

Also test DNS resolution from a diagnostic Pod to isolate the issue.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟲: 𝗜𝗻𝘃𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗴𝗮𝘁𝗲 𝗡𝗲𝘁𝘄𝗼𝗿𝗸𝗶𝗻𝗴

If CoreDNS is healthy but lookups still fail, investigate:

- NetworkPolicy rules
- CNI plugin health
- UDP/TCP port 53 connectivity
- Upstream DNS configuration
- Node-level networking issues

💡 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

A Running Pod does not guarantee DNS resolution is working.

Troubleshoot the complete DNS path:

Application Pod → DNS Configuration → CoreDNS Service → CoreDNS Pods → Upstream DNS (for external names).
